tasks.description:
PROBLEM. codex's wiki_pages already holds every page with parent_id (NULL = root) and a libs/fracindex position string (siblings ordered by position, then id). Page ids are referenced everywhere: backlinks, refs, attachments, drafts, the space's home page, links inside page content, and URLs. closure-tree only creates nodes through Add/GetOrAdd, which insert a NEW row with an auto-increment node_id. So the only migration path today is re-inserting every page under a new id and rewriting every reference. codex needs a one-shot "adopt" that builds closure-tree's tables for rows that already exist.

REQUIREMENT.
- An operation that, for one tenant, takes the existing rows of the node table (they already have node_id, tenant and the payload; codex renames id to node_id and fills the tenant column in its own migration) plus each node's parent and the sibling order, and writes:
  - the depth-0 self row and every ancestor row in the closure table;
  - the virtual-root row (ancestor 0, depth = the node's level) for every node;
  - sort_order spaced as Renormalize does (10, 20, 30, ... per sibling group, in the given order);
  - the meta rows for every parent.
- Parent and order input: either the names of legacy columns on the node table (e.g. legacy_parent_id, legacy_position, ordered by legacy_position then node_id) or a caller-provided list of (node_id, parent_id, order). The choice is open. It must be set-based (a recursive CTE, or level-by-level INSERT ... SELECT), not one Add per node, and must handle 50,000 nodes in one tenant.
- Validation runs before anything is written:
  - a parent that is not a node of the same tenant: configurable, either an error (default) or attach the node to the root (codex wants the root, matching RestorePage's behaviour for a missing parent);
  - a cycle: an error naming at least one node of the cycle;
  - nodes that already have closure rows: an error, unless a "rebuild" option first deletes the tenant's closure and meta rows.
- Runs inside the caller's transaction (R1), so codex can migrate wiki_pages, drop the legacy columns and commit atomically. Idempotent with the rebuild option.
- Document the required node-table columns (node_id as auto-increment primary key, tenant, sort_order, and no physical parent_id, see R7), plus a PostgreSQL note: rows copied into a new table with explicit ids need the node_id sequence advanced (setval) afterwards.

ACCEPTANCE (all dialects): import a forest with several roots, depth 5, 1,000 siblings under one parent, one dangling parent (both modes) and one cycle (error, nothing written). The closure, meta and sort_order rows match those Add would produce for the same shape (compare with a tree built by Add). Descendants, TreeDescendants, IsDescendant and Ancestors answer correctly. Later Add/Update/DeleteRecurse calls work. A rerun with rebuild gives identical rows.

DECISION (2026-11-20, Andrés): build it as a GENERAL operation, not a codex migration helper: "build the closure tables from parent pointers". The caller supplies (node_id, parent_id, order) per node, either as a list or as column names on the node table; node ids are kept; everything is validated before any write; a rebuild mode first wipes the tenant's closure and meta rows. Framed this way it doubles as a repair tool for a tenant whose closure rows drifted. Orphan policy is a generic option: error (default) or attach to the root. No codex vocabulary (fracindex, pages, legacy_*) in the API, README or tests; codex-specific ordering (fracindex position then id) is the caller's job when it builds the input.
